 fundraising edge and a July Democratic internal poll showing a one-point race signal real competitiveness", "A strong Democratic midterm environment is the main force pulling the race toward tossup territory"], "rationale": "The district's five-point Republican lean and Crank's incumbency outweigh a favorable midterm environment and Killin's fundraising advantage; raters uniformly put the race on the Republican side and markets price Killin near one-in-three, while the only poll — a July Democratic internal — shows just a one-point race.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling in the final weeks, or a stronger-than-expected Democratic wave, could push the race into genuine tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 69 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Jeff Crank (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 5th District, centered on Colorado Springs, leans Republican by about five points, and Representative Jeff Crank is seeking re-election against Democrat Jessica Killin. Nine race raters line up on the Republican side of the ledger — mostly Lean R, with one Tossup and one Likely R — and prediction markets give Killin roughly a one-in-three chance.\n\nThe race is closer than the district's usual margins. Killin has out-raised Crank, $3.1 million to $2.2 million, and the only public poll, a July survey from a Democratic firm, showed a one-point contest. Headlines from the August debates and dueling attack ads confirm a hard-fought campaign. Still, the partisan lean, incumbency, and the weight of expert ratings point to a modest Republican edge heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.332, "margin": -2.57}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3239, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3532, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.346, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2907, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3013, "margin": -3.0}, {"